      "data": {
        "id": "sd_1",
        "name": "Study Design",
        "instanceType": "InterventionalStudyDesign",
        "description": "Study AMCD is a Phase 3b, randomized, multicenter study to assess the efficacy and safety of mirikizumab and placebo compared with mirikizumab concomitantly administered with tirzepatide at the MTD (up to 15 mg) in adult participants with moderately to severely active UC and obesity, or overweight and in the presence of at least 1 weight-related comorbid condition. The maximum duration of this study is approximately 61 weeks, with the following study periods:  Study Period I, Screening: up to 5 weeks (≤35 days)  Study Period II, Induction and Dose Escalation: 24 weeks  Study Period III, Maintenance Treatment: 28 weeks, and  Safety follow-up: 4 weeks after the last visit. For randomization ratios and stratification factors, see Section 6.3.",
        "rationale": "Study rationale Concomitant administration of mirikizumab and tirzepatide in patients with UC with obesity or overweight is expected to improve gastroenterologic and/or metabolic outcomes compared with treatment with mirikizumab and placebo. Sample size Approximately 350 participants who meet entry criteria will be randomly assigned at Visit 2 in a 1:1 ratio to either the  mirikizumab and placebo treatment arm, or  mirikizumab and tirzepatide at the MTD (up to 15 mg) concomitant administration treatment arm. Refer to Section 9.5 for the rationale for the sample size. Choice of primary endpoint The multi-component primary endpoint at Week 52 includes clinical remission and at least 10% weight reduction. Clinical remission Clinical remission, as defined in Section 8.1, was chosen based on regulatory guidance from FDA (2016) and EMA (2018). Both recommend a composite endpoint for both symptomatic and endoscopic remission. At least 10% weight reduction A body weight reduction of at least 5% is considered the threshold for clinically meaningful effect on the basis of improvement in metabolic health. Greater weight reductions yield additional clinical benefits and may be required for improvement in certain weight-related",
